{#
  T-3.03 — the announcement marquee. TWO POSITIONS, ONE COMPONENT.

  Usage:
    {% include 'components.announcement-bar' %}                        {# top #}
    {% include 'components.announcement-bar' with { position: 'bottom' } %}

  Parameters:
    position  'top' (default) or 'bottom'

  SCOPE EXTENDED 2026-08-08 BY THE OWNER, after T-4.08's third-pass audit found
  the bar **drawn twice** on `Home Page (No Scroll).pdf`: once above the header
  and once between the partner banner and the footer, carrying the top bar's own
  copy at a different animation offset. The first pass built the upper one only.

  ONE TEXT, TWO SWITCHES. The artboard shows the same content in both bars, so
  there is one `announcement_text` and a merchant cannot let the two drift apart.
  What is independent is whether each bar exists, which is a placement decision
  and not a copy decision.

  NOT A REGISTERED HOME SECTION, and the backlog's "New sections: registered in
  twilight.json" is the one line of it not followed. A registered `home.*`
  component renders inside `<main>`, below the header. **The artboard puts this
  bar above the header**, so registering it would place it in the wrong half of
  the page in exchange for an ordering control that would only ever have one
  correct answer. It is theme settings instead — which is also what B6's ruling
  literally says: "announcement text is a theme setting". Recorded in
  /docs/DERIVED-DECISIONS.md.

  ONE STRING, NOT A LIST. The artboard shows two phrases — «كود خصم للتحويل
  البنكي AM5» and «من لحظة الطلب… إلى بابك دون انتظار.» — separated by space. The
  backlog specifies `announcement_text`, singular, so that is what this is: a
  merchant writes both phrases with a separator between them. A collection would
  have been a field the plan does not call for.

  READ ONCE, SCROLLED TWICE. A seamless marquee needs its content duplicated —
  the copy is what fills the gap the first one leaves as it exits. The duplicate
  is `aria-hidden`, so a screen reader reads the announcement exactly once, which
  is the acceptance criterion about repetition.

  It is not a live region and must not become one. The text does not change; it
  merely moves. Announcing it on a timer would interrupt whatever the user was
  reading, repeatedly, for no new information.
#}
{% set announcement_text = theme.settings.get('announcement_text') %}
{% set announcement_position = position|default('top') %}
{% set announcement_top_on = theme.settings.get('announcement_enabled', true) %}
{% set announcement_bottom_on = theme.settings.get('announcement_bottom_enabled', true) %}
{% set announcement_on = announcement_position == 'top' ? announcement_top_on : announcement_bottom_on %}

{#
  THE SECOND BAR IS SILENT WHEN THE FIRST ONE IS PRESENT, and this is the whole
  of what "readable by screen readers without repetition" means once there are
  two of them. Each bar already duplicates its own track for the seamless loop
  and hides the copy, so one bar reads once — but two bars read the same sentence
  twice on one page, which is the repetition the criterion is about, arriving by
  a new route. When the top bar is on, the bottom one is decoration and says so.

  **It is conditional and not absolute.** A merchant who runs only the lower bar
  has one announcement on the page and it must be readable; hiding it
  unconditionally would delete the information rather than de-duplicate it.
#}
{% set announcement_silent = announcement_position == 'bottom' and announcement_top_on %}

{% if announcement_on and announcement_text %}
    <div class="announcement-bar announcement-bar--{{ announcement_position }} card"
         {% if announcement_silent %}aria-hidden="true"{% endif %}>
        <div class="announcement-bar__track">
            <span class="announcement-bar__text">{{ announcement_text }}</span>
            <span class="announcement-bar__text" aria-hidden="true">{{ announcement_text }}</span>
        </div>
    </div>
{% endif %}
